#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""build-review — the record of a build's own review loop, which dispatch's build prompt asks each run to keep.

    build-review round <worktree> (--verdicts FILE | --found 0) --fixed N [--left N] [--check green|red] [--reviewers a,b] [--note TEXT] [--phase build|finish]
    build-review end   <worktree> --outcome clean|cap|held [--why TEXT] [--phase build|finish]
    build-review show  <worktree> [--phase build|finish]
    build-review gate  <worktree> --session ID
    build-review compare [--json]
    build-review covers <worktree> [--base REF]
    build-review picks <verdict file> <worktree>
    build-review cap
    build-review review <worktree> --cli codex|antigravity [--out DIR] [--base REF] [--origin MARKER]

Why (build-review, the owner, 2026-10-30): a build ended when its brief was done, and `/finish --unattended`
then reviewed, fixed and stopped on what it may not do; each stop cost the skipper a fix-round dispatch, a
queue wait and a new finish, 1-3 h each on the build Mac and 4-5 rounds on some branches. Since this step
the build loops review -> fix -> check -> fix in its own session until a round finds nothing to fix and the
check is green, at most the round cap's rounds (the prompt's words: dispatch's build prompt, the build-review
paragraph after its Steps: line). `/finish` keeps its
own review (the owner: "for now I'm ok with double review"); this record is the data a later decision on
that second review reads (docs/task-shape-2026-10-29.md, phase 2).

Where: `<git dir>/claude/build-review.json`, per worktree, beside autofinish.json (the git dir read back
from git, never built). One object: branch, session ($CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID),
repo (the main checkout), project (its folder name, review-gate.jsonl's `project`), worktree (review-gate's
`repo`), rounds [{round, tip, at, reviewed, reviewers, raised, found, open_s1, fixed, left, check, note}], `phase` finish
on a landing's own loop (below), and once `end` ran:
ended, outcome, why, tip. `found` counts the real S1, S2 and WRONG findings, S3s and those judged not real go
in `note`. A round's `tip` is HEAD when it is recorded: the round's fixes included.

Real is the verifier's word, never the builder's (verified-rounds, the owner, 2026-11-02: "make sure we're not
chasing a wild goose"; docs/verified-rounds-2026-11-02.md). The log until then: one loop that capped on 13, 9, 14, 6 and 15 "real" findings a round,
S3 fixes counted as found, and one loop that ran five rounds on S2s its own notes called not real. So a count
above 0 comes only from `--verdicts FILE`, the report of a fresh reviewer that raised none of them, given only
the round's S1, S2 and WRONG items: `found` is its blocks with `verdict: WRONG` and evidence (wrapped lines read,
one that says there is none not), a head tagged `[S3]` left out; `raised` is every verdict block it wrote; `open_s1`
its `UNVERIFIABLE` blocks with an `[S1]` head, which `covers` will not vouch past. `--found` takes 0 alone (nothing to verify, or
a prose-only round). And a round past the first is granted only when the round before confirmed a real finding
or its check was red: a round that confirmed nothing, with the check green or not recorded, is the loop's end
(exit 4, asked before the cap's exit 3). A held loop reopened by the answer, or a round that left items, is granted. The
builder still writes the file it hands over; this stops a loop that grades itself, not one that lies.

The last review reads the tip it hands over (landing-fixes-in-run, the owner, 2026-11-04: a loop "clean in 2 rounds"
whose last review read e99f2821 while the tip carried round 2's comment change and the landing's two test fixes, so
`covers` said no and the finish's whole review ran on a gated path). This is the loop's own rule, not left to
`covers`: each round keeps `reviewed`, the head of the newest review-gate line of its phase for this project and
branch when it is recorded (the commit that round's review read); a round after one that confirmed nothing with the
check green is still granted when the code moved past that head (a fix commit after the review, an S3 fixed in
passing), and a clean end is refused (exit 2) while the code at HEAD differs from what the last recorded round's
review read (never a review logged after that round, whose findings no round verified), or while the worktree holds an
uncommitted change. "The code" is every path but PROSE_RE's (docs/, TODO.md, a CHANGELOG): a step's done stamp or a
CHANGELOG line after the last review needs no round; in a repo with `in_place` (the vault) a finish counts prose too.
A finish round with no head is no clean end (its review was not read), and the landing's own session never writes the
build's record (autofinish.json names it). A head git cannot read counts as moved. A round on a branch with no review-gate line of
its phase (a prose-only loop) has no `reviewed`, and grants and ends as before; the lookup has no lower bound, as
`covers`' does. At the cap no round is left to review a move: the build ends clean anyway (`covers` then sends /finish
to review it, as before this rule), a finish's loop only on a tests-only move (`covers`' own rule), else cap.

`--phase finish` (the same step): the landing's own loop, which /finish --unattended runs when its review finds real
items (the finish skill, step 1: review, verify, fix, check, round, at most the same cap, the same verifier's file),
so a landing fixes what it can in its run and lands, rather than stopping for a fix round queued as a new build. Its
record is `<git dir>/claude/build-review-finish.json`, beside the build's, which it never replaces: `covers` and `gate`
read the build's alone, and a finish loop's line in the log (`phase` finish) is never a build's for `covers`' fallback
or `compare`'s rows (it counts as the row's `finish_rounds`, `finish_found` and `finish_fixed`). Its `reviewed` heads
are read from `phase` finish review-gate lines. One loop per session holds per phase.

`round` refuses a round past the cap with exit 3 and the cap's words, and `end` then takes
`--outcome cap`. A record already ended, or kept by another session (a run that died before `end`), is
replaced by a fresh one at the next `round` or `end`, so a fix round after a stopped landing gets its own
rounds up to the cap, and another session's unended record is logged with outcome `died` first, so no rounds are lost. Once a
session ended its record clean or cap, its next `round` or `end` is refused (exit 2): one loop and one log line per
session. A record ended `held` (a D-item relayed) is reopened by that session's next `round`, rounds kept against
the cap, and its next end is a second line for the same (session, started): a reader keeps the last. A clean end
is refused (exit 2) unless the last round found nothing with the check green. `end` also appends the whole record as one JSON line to the durable
log, $BUILD_REVIEW_LOG, else review-gate.jsonl's folder ($REVIEW_GATE_LOG's, else plumb's usage-report
data folder, else the core's log dir): a worktree goes away at its landing, and the log is what the decision reads. Join it to
review-gate.jsonl by project and branch; that log also holds a line for each build round's own merge, so
the finish's lines are those after the last line's `ended` for that project and branch (each step of a
branch keeps its own record).

`gate` is yolo_launch's read (dispatch): exit 1 with the reason when session ID's own loop stopped (cap,
held) or recorded rounds and never ended, so no launch starts on unfixed findings whatever the run's last
line said (the build-review finish review: the prompt alone guarded it); exit 0 when the record is clean,
absent, or another session's (a run that opted out, or a loop an earlier run kept).

A call needs $CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID: without one every caller would read as the same session, and one
run's end would refuse every later run on the worktree (exit 2; the same review).

`compare` is the read docs/finish-second-review-2026-11-02.md decides from (is /finish's second review worth it on a build-reviewed tip?): per
project and branch with a record in the log, the build's rounds, found and fixed, its outcomes; the finish's
review-gate lines (`phase` finish, or, on a line written before review-gate's --phase, one after the last
`ended`), their raw S1, S2 and WRONG; and the review agents' spend from usage-report's call rows beside the logs
(`<data>/<host>/calls/*.csv`), split at the last `ended` into the build's and the finish's. Raw counts are the
reviewers' own, before anyone judged them: whether a finish finding was real is read from what the landing
did with it (its PR), never from here.

`covers` is /finish step 1's read (docs/finish-second-review-2026-11-02.md's D1-B, the owner, 2026-11-02: the finish's second review found no S1
on four build-reviewed landings): exit 0 when the finish may skip its review because the build's own loop already
reviewed this code. All of: the tree is clean (a fix the finish has not committed yet is unreviewed); the worktree's
record (else, a record lost on this host, the log's newest ended line for this project and branch — the log is
per host, so a build that ran on another host is never covered here) ended `clean`, never cap, held or died, and was
kept by another session than the one asking (a finish never vouches for itself); review-gate.jsonl holds a
`phase` build line for this project and branch by the loop's end, and the newest one's `head`, the commit the loop's last
review read, carries the same code change against --base (default origin/main) as HEAD: a hash of `git diff
<merge-base> <commit>`, whitespace kept, blob ids and hunk line numbers dropped, PROSE_RE's paths (docs/, TODO.md,
a CHANGELOG) left out on both sides — or, when only a `tests/` file outside the suite runners moved since that head,
the same hash with TESTS_RE's paths left out too (a landing's check fix; TESTS_RE's comment says why); and
`review-gate plan` names no principal build. So a code fix committed after
the last review, a fix the finish made outside tests/, a main merged in again, or a rebase that conflicted all change
it, and the review runs; /finish asks again after its step 11.1 rebase. Never in a repo whose .claude/workflow.json has
`in_place` (the vault), read at --base and in the main checkout, never the branch's copy. Exit 1 says why not.

`picks` is the finish loop's read of a verifier's report (/finish step 1, unattended; landing-fixes-in-run): one line
per real finding (a `verdict:` block as `round --verdicts` counts it) — `fix <head>` for a fix that is no choice (no
`➤ Recommended:` line, no options named, no Reversible mark in its block), `take <head> — <pick>` for a choice the run
takes itself, `stop <head> — <why>` for one it may not, or a choice with options and no pick named. A pick is taken
when it is marked `Reversible: yes` standing alone (on its Recommended line, else the first line-start mark after it,
keel's rule; two marks that disagree stop), it names one of the block's listed options by its letter (`A`, `(a)`,
`Option 1`) with no hedge after it (`if`, `unless`, `once`, `but` …), it is no conditional pick, the block bar its head and its evidence holds none of keel's held classes (options the evidence lists are read too; HELD, kept equal to keel's DND_HOLDS by the suite: money, a deletion, a public
publish, a third party's answer), as keel's `dnd_held` judges `yolo --dnd` picks;
`Reversible: no`, unmarked, a conditional pick or a held class is a
stop. Exit 0 when nothing stops, 5 when a line says stop, 2 when the file has no verdict block.

`cap` prints the round cap: `wharf-runs/build-review-rounds` in the config dir, a whole number from 1 to 20, else
DEFAULT_ROUNDS, read at each call so a changed key holds from the next round (docs/finish-second-review-2026-11-02.md's D2-B; the owner,
2026-11-01: a limit changes without a build). It is the trial read `regatta limit` is to run once live-limits lands.

Exit: 0 written or shown; 1 `gate` refuses; 5 `picks` holds a stop; 2 a usage error, no session id, a worktree that is no git
checkout, an `end` already made, or an end whose log line could not be written (the record stays open, so
the end can be made again), a count above 0 without a verdict file, or a verdict file with no block; 3 the
round cap; 4 a round not granted (the one before confirmed nothing with the check green).
"""
import argparse
import functools
import hashlib
import json
import os
import re
import subprocess
import sys
import time
from pathlib import Path

DEFAULT_ROUNDS = 3   # docs/finish-second-review-2026-11-02.md's D2-B (the owner, 2026-11-02): 5 until then; three of eight loops capped still finding
ROUNDS_KEY = "wharf-runs/build-review-rounds"
# what `covers` leaves out of the code change: only what a loop and a finish write by design (docs, TODO.md, a
# CHANGELOG). Never review-gate's DOC_RE, which calls every .md and .txt prose: a skill, an agent, the publish gate's
# allowlist and exemptions are behaviour (the landing review, 2026-11-02)
PROSE_RE = re.compile(r"(^docs/|^TODO\.md$|(^|/)CHANGELOG\.md$)")
# what `covers` may also leave out when it alone moved since the last review: a module's tests/, bar the suite runners
# (landing-fixes-in-run, the brief's third item: claim-after-failed-start's landing fixed two lighthouse cases for its
# check, cf94fb3d and a1e95421, and the finish's whole review then ran on a gated path for them). Yes, because a file
# under tests/ ships no behaviour to a live tool: no hook, shim or tool loads one (classes.sh's class 1 walks bin, hooks,
# lib and libexec), and every pull request's CI runs the whole check line on it before `pr merge`. It does run on the
# Macs, in every session's check, so only a suite itself is vouched past (`vouchable`): never a shared fixture another
# suite sources (a config fixture keeps the suites off the live config and HOME), a lint or stub a runner calls (the
# core's home_rm.py), a fixtures/ file, or anything when the exclude list does not read (the landing review). The cost: a weakened
# assertion, or a suite silenced from inside (an early `exit 0`), lands unreviewed; CI runs it but trusts its exit, so
# the next build's review of that suite is what reads it. Never `tests/check.sh`
# (or a `check.sh` anywhere under tests/): the runner decides which suites run, so a change there can silence a
# whole suite, as no single case can; nor a tests/ path on the base's `autofinish_exclude` (classes.sh, the list's own
# gate, which the check line runs: the round-1 verifier's finding), read at the base as `in_place` is. Never in the
# build's own loop either (its final-tip rule above): a build fixes
# its tests in the session that reviews them, and one more round there is cheap.
TESTS_RE = re.compile(r"(^|/)tests/")
RUNNER_RE = re.compile(r"(^|/)tests/(.*/)?check\.sh$")
RECORD = "build-review.json"
FINISH_RECORD = "build-review-finish.json"   # --phase finish: the landing's own loop, beside the build's
PHASES = ("build", "finish")
OUTCOMES = ("clean", "cap", "held")
HERE = Path(__file__).resolve().parent


def git(wt, *args):
    r = subprocess.run(["git", "-C", str(wt), *args], capture_output=True, text=True)
    return r.stdout.strip() if r.returncode == 0 else None


def where(wt, phase="build"):
    """(record path, branch, main checkout) for the worktree and phase, or exit 2."""
    gd = git(wt, "rev-parse", "--absolute-git-dir")
    if not gd:
        print("build-review: %s is no git checkout" % wt, file=sys.stderr)
        sys.exit(2)
    common_dir = git(wt, "rev-parse", "--path-format=absolute", "--git-common-dir") or gd
    repo = os.path.dirname(common_dir) if common_dir.endswith("/.git") else common_dir
    return (Path(gd) / "claude" / (FINISH_RECORD if phase == "finish" else RECORD),
            git(wt, "rev-parse", "--abbrev-ref", "HEAD") or "", repo)


def load(path):
    try:
        d = json.loads(path.read_text())
        return d if isinstance(d, dict) else None
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        return None


def save(path, d):
    path.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
    tmp = path.with_name(path.name + ".tmp")
    tmp.write_text(json.dumps(d, indent=2) + "\n")
    os.replace(tmp, path)


def log_path():
    """review-gate.jsonl's folder, found as review-gate's log_path() finds it, so the two logs sit together."""
    if os.environ.get("BUILD_REVIEW_LOG"):
        return Path(os.environ["BUILD_REVIEW_LOG"])
    if os.environ.get("REVIEW_GATE_LOG"):
        return Path(os.environ["REVIEW_GATE_LOG"]).parent / "build-review.jsonl"
    try:
        sys.path.insert(0, str(HERE.parent / "lib"))
        import core_path  # noqa: PLC0415
        core_path.add()
        import common  # noqa: PLC0415
    except Exception:  # noqa: BLE001 — no core: the core's default log dir
        return Path.home() / ".local" / "state" / "regatta" / "build-review.jsonl"
    try:
        from importlib.machinery import SourceFileLoader  # noqa: PLC0415
        ur = common.tool("usage-report")
        if ur:
            return Path(SourceFileLoader("usage_report", str(ur)).load_module().data_dir()) / "build-review.jsonl"
    except Exception:  # noqa: BLE001 — review-gate's own fallback: the core's log dir
        pass
    return Path(common.LOG_DIR) / "build-review.jsonl"


def rounds_cap():
    """The round cap (the header's `cap`); a value that does not read says so on stderr and is the default."""
    try:
        sys.path.insert(0, str(HERE.parent / "lib"))
        import core_path  # noqa: PLC0415
        core_path.add()
        import common  # noqa: PLC0415
        rows, why = common.config_read(ROUNDS_KEY)
    except Exception:  # noqa: BLE001 — no core: the default
        return DEFAULT_ROUNDS
    if not rows and not why:
        return DEFAULT_ROUNDS
    # a value split one word per line reads as one, a note after # or ( as nothing (AGENTS.md Traps)
    w = re.split("[#(]", " ".join(rows or []), maxsplit=1)[0].split()
    if not why and len(w) == 1 and w[0].isascii() and w[0].isdecimal() and 1 <= int(w[0]) <= 20:
        return int(w[0])
    print("build-review: %s %s — the default, %d" % (ROUNDS_KEY, why or "is no whole number from 1 to 20", DEFAULT_ROUNDS),
          file=sys.stderr)
    return DEFAULT_ROUNDS


def gated_paths(wt, base, repo=None):
    """The `.claude/workflow.json` `autofinish_exclude` entries (a file, or a folder ending /) at base and in the main
    checkout (sync.py writes it there untracked for some repos), as in_place reads both; () when neither has the file.
    None when it does not read — a base git cannot resolve, a file that is there and does not parse, a list that is
    no list — so a caller vouches past nothing (a narrowing setting fails closed: AGENTS.md Traps; the landing review)."""
    if git(wt, "rev-parse", "--verify", "-q", "%s^{commit}" % base) is None:
        return None
    texts = []
    if git(wt, "cat-file", "-e", "%s:.claude/workflow.json" % base) is not None:
        t = git(wt, "show", "%s:.claude/workflow.json" % base)
        if t is None:
            return None
        texts.append(t)
    if repo:
        try:
            texts.append((Path(repo) / ".claude" / "workflow.json").read_text())
        except FileNotFoundError:
            pass
        except OSError:
            return None
    out = []
    for t in texts:
        try:
            wf = json.loads(t)
        except ValueError:
            return None
        ex = wf.get("autofinish_exclude") if isinstance(wf, dict) else None
        if ex is None:
            continue
        if not isinstance(ex, list):
            return None
        out += [e for e in ex if isinstance(e, str) and e]
    return tuple(out)


def base_ref(wt, repo):
    """The repo's main as a ref here: `origin/<main>` when fetched, else `<main>` (workflow.json's `main`, else main)."""
    m = "main"
    try:
        wf = json.loads((Path(repo) / ".claude" / "workflow.json").read_text())
        if isinstance(wf, dict) and isinstance(wf.get("main"), str) and wf["main"]:
            m = wf["main"]
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        pass
    return "origin/" + m if git(wt, "rev-parse", "--verify", "-q", "origin/%s^{commit}" % m) else m


SUITE_RE = re.compile(r"(^|/)tests/[^/]+\.(?:sh|py|m?js)$")


@functools.lru_cache(maxsize=None)
def vouchable(wt, name, gated, tip="HEAD"):
    """True when covers may vouch past a change to this tests/ path (TESTS_RE's comment): a suite itself, directly in a
    tests/ folder, that exists at tip and that folder's runner runs (named, or matched by its glob) — never a runner, a
    gated path, a fixture folder's file, a helper (a lint such as home_rm.py, a stub, a sitecustomize, a script run by
    hand), a deleted suite (a runner's glob drops it silently), nor a file another tests/ file sources or imports (a
    shared config fixture keeps every suite off the live config and HOME: the landing review). Anything git cannot
    read is not vouchable."""
    if gated is None or not TESTS_RE.search(name) or RUNNER_RE.search(name) or is_gated(name, gated):
        return False
    if not SUITE_RE.search(name) or git(wt, "cat-file", "-e", "%s:%s" % (tip, name)) is None:
        return False
    base = os.path.basename(name)
    stem = base.rsplit(".", 1)[0]
    if base.endswith(".py") and not stem.startswith("test_"):
        return False
    runner = git(wt, "show", "%s:%s" % (tip, os.path.join(os.path.dirname(name), "check.sh")))
    ext = base.rsplit(".", 1)[1]
    if runner is None or not (re.search(r"(?<![\w.-])%s\b" % re.escape(base), runner)
                              or re.search(r"\*\.%s\b" % re.escape(ext), runner)):
        return False
    r = subprocess.run(["git", "-C", str(wt), "grep", "-z", "-l", "-F", "-e", stem, tip, "--", ":(glob)**/tests/**"],
                       capture_output=True)
    if r.returncode not in (0, 1):
        return False
    sourced = re.compile(r"^\s*(?:\.|source)\s+[^#\n;&|]*?%s\b" % re.escape(base))
    imported = re.compile(r"^\s*(?:from\s+%s\s+import\b|import\s+%s\b)|%s\.py['\"]?\s*\)?\s*\.load_module" % (
        (re.escape(stem),) * 3))
    for hit in r.stdout.decode("utf-8", "surrogateescape").split("\0"):
        other = hit.split(":", 1)[1] if ":" in hit else hit
        if not other or other == name:
            continue
        text = git(wt, "show", "%s:%s" % (tip, other))
        if text is None:
            return False
        rx = imported if other.endswith(".py") else sourced
        if any(rx.search(line) for line in text.splitlines() if not line.lstrip().startswith("#")):
            return False
    return True


def is_gated(name, gated):
    return any(name == e or (e.endswith("/") and name.startswith(e)) for e in gated)


def code_patch(wt, base, tip, tests=True, gated=()):
    """A hash of the branch's code change at tip against base (PROSE_RE's paths left out, and TESTS_RE's bar the
    runners when tests is False); "" for none, None when
    git cannot tell. The diff text itself, never `git patch-id --stable`, which ignores whitespace and so passed a
    Python re-indent or a space inside a string as the same change (the landing review, 2026-11-02): only the
    `index` lines (blob ids) and the hunk headers' line numbers are dropped, so a clean rebase onto a moved main
    keeps the hash. Names are read NUL-separated and given back literally: a quoted non-ASCII name matched nothing."""
    mb = git(wt, "merge-base", base, tip)
    if not mb:
        return None
    r = subprocess.run(["git", "-C", str(wt), "diff", "-z", "--name-only", "--no-renames", mb, tip], capture_output=True)
    if r.returncode:
        return None
    # which tests/ paths are left out is judged at HEAD for both tips, so the head's hash and the tip's compare alike
    code = [n for n in r.stdout.decode("utf-8", "surrogateescape").split("\0") if n and not PROSE_RE.search(n)
            and (tests or not vouchable(wt, n, gated))]
    if not code:
        return ""
    diff = subprocess.run(["git", "--literal-pathspecs", "-C", str(wt), "diff", "--no-color", "--no-ext-diff",
                           "--no-renames", mb, tip, "--", *code], capture_output=True)
    if diff.returncode:
        return None
    h = hashlib.sha256()
    for line in diff.stdout.split(b"\n"):
        if line.startswith(b"index "):
            continue
        if line.startswith(b"@@ "):
            line = b"@@"
        h.update(line + b"\n")
    return h.hexdigest()


def in_place(wt, repo, base):
    """True when the repo is the vault: .claude/workflow.json's `in_place` at base, or in the main checkout (sync.py
    writes it there untracked for some repos) — never the branch's own copy, which the branch could edit (review-gate
    reads it at the base too). A file that is there and does not read counts as in_place."""
    texts = [git(wt, "show", "%s:.claude/workflow.json" % base)]
    try:
        texts.append((Path(repo) / ".claude" / "workflow.json").read_text())
    except OSError:
        texts.append(None)
    for t in texts:
        if t is None:
            continue
        try:
            wf = json.loads(t)
        except ValueError:
            return True
        if not isinstance(wf, dict) or wf.get("in_place"):
            return True
    return False


def build_reviewed_head(project, branch, until, phase="build"):
    """The head of the newest review-gate line of this phase for project and branch logged by `until` (epoch, the
    loop's end), or None: the commit the loop's last review actually read. No lower bound: a round's review is logged
    before its `round` call, the first one before the record exists, and an older review whose head carries the
    same code reviewed that code too."""
    gate = Path(os.environ["REVIEW_GATE_LOG"]) if os.environ.get("REVIEW_GATE_LOG") else log_path().parent / "review-gate.jsonl"
    best = None
    for g in jsonl(gate):
        t = epoch(g.get("ts"))
        if (g.get("phase") == phase and g.get("project") == project and g.get("branch") == branch and g.get("head")
                and t is not None and t <= until and (best is None or t >= best[0])):
            best = (t, g["head"])
    return best[1] if best else None


def tests_only_move(wt, head, repo, tip="HEAD"):
    """True when every path outside PROSE_RE that differs between head and tip is a tests/ file `covers` may vouch past
    (vouchable: a suite, no runner, no fixture, none on autofinish_exclude, read at the repo's main as base_ref names
    it, and nothing when that list does not read)."""
    if not head:
        return False
    r = subprocess.run(["git", "-C", str(wt), "diff", "-z", "--name-only", "--no-renames", head, tip], capture_output=True)
    if r.returncode:
        return False
    gated = gated_paths(wt, base_ref(wt, repo), repo)
    if gated is None:
        return False
    names = [n for n in r.stdout.decode("utf-8", "surrogateescape").split("\0") if n and not PROSE_RE.search(n)]
    return all(vouchable(wt, n, gated) for n in names)


def dirty_paths(wt, prose=False):
    """The worktree's uncommitted paths (staged, unstaged, untracked) outside PROSE_RE (every one when prose), or None
    when git cannot tell: a fix left uncommitted is code no review read (the landing review)."""
    r = subprocess.run(["git", "-C", str(wt), "status", "--porcelain", "-z", "--untracked-files=all"], capture_output=True)
    if r.returncode:
        return None
    out, parts = [], r.stdout.decode("utf-8", "surrogateescape").split("\0")
    i = 0
    while i < len(parts):
        e = parts[i]
        i += 1
        if len(e) < 4:
            continue
        if e[0] in "RC" or e[1] in "RC":   # a rename's source follows as its own NUL field
            i += 1
        if prose or not PROSE_RE.search(e[3:]):
            out.append(e[3:])
    return out


def code_moved(wt, head, tip="HEAD", prose=False):
    """True when a path outside PROSE_RE (any path, when prose: the vault, where prose is what the gate reviews)
    differs between head (what a review read) and tip; False when none does; None when git cannot tell (a head this
    checkout lacks). The header's final-tip rule."""
    if not head or git(wt, "cat-file", "-e", head + "^{commit}") is None:
        return None
    r = subprocess.run(["git", "-C", str(wt), "diff", "-z", "--name-only", "--no-renames", head, tip], capture_output=True)
    if r.returncode:
        return None
    return any(n and (prose or not PROSE_RE.search(n)) for n in r.stdout.decode("utf-8", "surrogateescape").split("\0"))


def branch_moved(wt, head, base, prose=False):
    """code_moved, bar a clean merge of base: `check --ci` merges a moved main into a build branch, and the branch's own
    change against base (code_patch, covers' test) is then what the review read, so no new round is owed for it. A merge
    resolved by hand changes that change, and still counts. The vault (prose) keeps code_moved's whole reading."""
    m = code_moved(wt, head, prose=prose)
    if m is True and not prose and base:
        then, now = code_patch(wt, base, head), code_patch(wt, base, "HEAD")
        if then is not None and then == now:
            return False
    return m


def covers(wt, base):
    """(True, why) when the finish may skip its review (the header's `covers`), else (False, why)."""
    path, branch, repo = where(wt)
    project = os.path.basename(repo)
    if in_place(wt, repo, base):
        return False, "a repo with in_place: every landing is reviewed, prose included"
    if git(wt, "status", "--porcelain") != "":
        return False, "the worktree has uncommitted changes: commit them, then ask again"
    d, src = load(path), "this worktree's record"
    if d and d.get("branch") != branch:
        d = None
    ended = [x for x in jsonl(log_path()) if x.get("project") == project and x.get("branch") == branch
             and x.get("ended") and x.get("phase", "build") == "build"]   # a finish loop's line vouches for nothing
    # A carried vendor loop can be newer than this owner's old local build
    # record. That old Claude loop must not mask the vendor review policy.
    newest = sorted(enumerate(ended), key=lambda x: (x[1]["ended"], x[0]))[-1][1] if ended else None
    if newest and (not d or (newest.get("agent") in ("codex", "antigravity")
                             and newest["ended"] >= (d.get("ended") or d.get("started") or 0))):
        d, src = newest, "the log's newest line"
    if not d:
        return False, "no build-review record for %s" % branch
    vendor = d.get("agent") in ("codex", "antigravity") or any(
        set(r.get("reviewers") or []) & {"codex", "antigravity"} for r in d.get("rounds") or [])
    if vendor:
        try:
            sys.path.insert(0, str(HERE.parent / "lib"))
            import core_path
            core_path.add()
            import common
            rows, bad = common.config_read("wharf-runs/trust-vendor-review")
        except Exception:
            rows, bad = None, True
        if bad or rows != ["on"]:
            return False, "vendor review credit is off (wharf-runs/trust-vendor-review): /finish runs its full Claude review"
    if not d.get("ended"):
        return False, "%s never ended (%d round(s))" % (src, len(d.get("rounds") or []))
    if d.get("outcome") != "clean":
        return False, "%s ended %s" % (src, d.get("outcome"))
    me = os.environ.get("CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID")
    if me and d.get("session") == me:
        return False, "%s was kept by this session: a finish never vouches for itself" % src
    rounds = d.get("rounds") or []
    if not rounds:
        return False, "%s ended clean with no round" % src
    open_s1 = sum(r.get("open_s1") or 0 for r in rounds)
    if open_s1:
        # an S1 its verifier could neither confirm nor refute is no finding the loop fixes, and no code a review cleared;
        # a later round's review never re-verifies it, so any round's counts (the landing review)
        return False, "%s's rounds left %d S1 its verifier called UNVERIFIABLE" % (src, open_s1)
    head = build_reviewed_head(project, branch, (d.get("ended") or 0) + 60)
    if not head:
        return False, "no build-phase review-gate line with a head for %s: nothing shows which code a review read" % branch
    if git(wt, "cat-file", "-e", head + "^{commit}") is None:
        return False, "the last reviewed head %s is not in this checkout" % head[:9]
    then, now = code_patch(wt, base, head), code_patch(wt, base, "HEAD")
    if then is None or now is None:
        return False, "git could not diff against %s" % base
    tests_only = False
    if then != now:
        # a landing's check fix: only tests/ moved since the review (TESTS_RE's comment says why that is no new code)
        gated = gated_paths(wt, base, repo)
        if gated is None:
            return False, "the code changed since the build's last review read it at %s, and the base's autofinish_exclude does not read" % head[:9]
        t_then = code_patch(wt, base, head, tests=False, gated=gated)
        t_now = code_patch(wt, base, "HEAD", tests=False, gated=gated)
        if t_then is None or t_now is None or t_then != t_now:
            return False, "the code changed since the build's last review read it at %s" % head[:9]
        tests_only = True
    try:
        plan = json.loads(subprocess.run([str(review_gate()), "plan", "--json", "--base", base],
                                         cwd=str(wt), capture_output=True, text=True).stdout)
    except (OSError, ValueError, TypeError):
        return False, "review-gate plan did not read, so whether the build is principal is unknown"
    if not isinstance(plan, dict) or plan.get("principal"):
        return False, "a principal build: its top-tier review runs at any size"
    return True, "the build's loop ended clean after %d round(s), its last review read %s, and the code against %s is the same%s" % (
        len(rounds), head[:9], base, " bar tests/ (a check fix since)" if tests_only else "")


def review_gate():
    """wharf's review-gate ($BUILD_REVIEW_GATE, the tests' stand-in), through the core's tool() where there is one,
    else beside this module in the repo."""
    if os.environ.get("BUILD_REVIEW_GATE"):
        return Path(os.environ["BUILD_REVIEW_GATE"])
    try:
        sys.path.insert(0, str(HERE.parent / "lib"))
        import core_path  # noqa: PLC0415
        core_path.add()
        import common  # noqa: PLC0415
        t = common.tool("review-gate")
        if t:
            return Path(t)
    except Exception:  # noqa: BLE001
        pass
    return HERE.parent.parent / "wharf" / "bin" / "review-gate"


def log(d, path=None):
    """One JSON line of the record in the durable log; True once written. A failure says so."""
    lp = log_path()
    try:
        lp.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
        with open(lp, "a", encoding="utf-8") as f:
            f.write(json.dumps(d, sort_keys=True) + "\n")
        return True
    except OSError as err:
        print("build-review: the log line was not written (%s)%s" % (err, "; the record is in %s" % path if path else ""),
              file=sys.stderr)
        return False


REVIEWERS = ("reviewer", "reviewer-high", "code-reviewer")


def jsonl(path):
    out = []
    try:
        lines = open(path, errors="replace").read().splitlines()
    except OSError:
        return out
    for line in lines:
        try:
            d = json.loads(line)
        except ValueError:
            continue
        if isinstance(d, dict):
            out.append(d)
    return out


def epoch(ts):
    """review-gate's `ts` (%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%S%z) or a call row's ISO time, as seconds; None when it will not read."""
    from datetime import datetime  # noqa: PLC0415
    for f in ("%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%S%z",):
        try:
            return datetime.strptime(ts, f).timestamp()
        except (TypeError, ValueError):
            pass
    try:
        return datetime.fromisoformat(str(ts).replace("Z", "+00:00")).timestamp()
    except ValueError:
        return None


def compare(folder):
    """One row per (project, branch) the build-review log names, oldest first; see the header."""
    import csv  # noqa: PLC0415
    import glob  # noqa: PLC0415
    builds, last = {}, {}
    for d in jsonl(folder / "build-review.jsonl"):   # a held loop reopened logs again: keep its last line
        if d.get("project") and d.get("branch") and d.get("ended"):   # the phase in the key: a finish loop is its own
            # a line with no start (none before build-review kept one) is its own loop
            last[(d["project"], d["branch"], d.get("session"), d.get("started") or ("ended", d["ended"]),
                  d.get("phase", "build"))] = d
    fins = [d for d in last.values() if d.get("phase") == "finish"]
    for d in last.values():
        if d.get("phase") == "finish":   # the landing's own loop: counted on its build's row below, never a row
            continue
        k = (d.get("project"), d.get("branch"))
        b = builds.setdefault(k, {"project": k[0], "branch": k[1], "rounds": 0, "found": 0, "fixed": 0, "outcomes": [],
                                  "ended": 0})
        b["rounds"] += len(d.get("rounds") or [])
        b["found"] += sum(r.get("found") or 0 for r in d.get("rounds") or [])
        b["fixed"] += sum(r.get("fixed") or 0 for r in d.get("rounds") or [])
        b["outcomes"].append(d.get("outcome"))
        b["ended"] = max(b["ended"], d["ended"])
    gate = Path(os.environ["REVIEW_GATE_LOG"]) if os.environ.get("REVIEW_GATE_LOG") else folder / "review-gate.jsonl"
    for b in builds.values():
        b.update(finish=[], spend={"build": 0.0, "finish": 0.0}, finish_rounds=0, finish_found=0, finish_fixed=0)
    for d in fins:
        b = builds.get((d.get("project"), d.get("branch")))
        if b:
            b["finish_rounds"] += len(d.get("rounds") or [])
            b["finish_found"] += sum(r.get("found") or 0 for r in d.get("rounds") or [])
            b["finish_fixed"] += sum(r.get("fixed") or 0 for r in d.get("rounds") or [])
    for g in jsonl(gate):
        b = builds.get((g.get("project"), g.get("branch")))
        t = epoch(g.get("ts"))
        if not b or g.get("phase") == "build" or (g.get("phase") != "finish" and (t is None or t <= b["ended"])):
            continue
        f, w = g.get("findings") or {}, g.get("wrong") or {}
        b["finish"].append({"ts": g.get("ts"), "at": t, "S1": f.get("S1", 0), "S2": f.get("S2", 0), "S3": f.get("S3", 0),
                            "S?": f.get("S?", 0),   # what a merge that did not run left unsorted
                            "wrong": sum(v or 0 for v in w.values()), "skipped": g.get("skipped")})
    for p in sorted(glob.glob(str(folder / "*" / "calls" / "*.csv"))):
        try:
            rows = list(csv.DictReader(open(p, errors="replace")))
        except OSError:
            continue
        for r in rows:
            b = builds.get((r.get("project"), r.get("branch")))
            if not b or r.get("agent") not in REVIEWERS:
                continue
            t = epoch(r.get("first"))
            try:
                usd = float(r.get("cost") or 0)
            except ValueError:
                continue
            b["spend"]["finish" if t is not None and t > b["ended"] else "build"] += usd
    out = sorted(builds.values(), key=lambda b: b["ended"])
    for b in out:
        b["spend"] = {k: round(v, 2) for k, v in b["spend"].items()}
        b["finish"].sort(key=lambda f: f["at"] or 0)   # the last is the newest pass, whatever the log's order
    return out


VERDICT_RE = re.compile(r"(?i)^\W*verdict\W*:\W*([A-Z][A-Z-]*)")   # review-gate's: a block is anchored on it
EVIDENCE_RE = re.compile(r"(?i)^\W*evidence\W*:\W*(.*)")
# a field label ends the one before; `➤ Recommended:` and `Reversible:` too, which the finish's verifier writes after
# `fix:`, or a report with no blank line between blocks reads the next block's head as this one's line
FIELD_RE = re.compile(r"(?i)^\W*(verdict|evidence|fix|why|severity|claim|recommended|reversible)\W*:")
# the severity tag in the head, after its dash (`file:line — [S3] claim`), never a tag the claim merely mentions
HEAD_SEV_RE = re.compile(r"(?:—|--|\s-)\s*\[\s*(S[123?])\s*\]")
# an evidence line that only says there is none: a theory, never a real finding (the reviewer's own words)
NO_EVIDENCE_RE = re.compile(r"(?i)^\W*(none|n/?a|nothing|no evidence|a theory|theory|not (probed|shown|run|reproduced|traced))\b")


def _head(lines, i):
    """Index of the head line of the block whose verdict sits at line i: back past blank lines and fences, then
    up the paragraph to its first line (a head that wraps keeps its tag on that first line)."""
    j = i - 1
    while j >= 0 and (not lines[j].strip() or lines[j].strip().startswith("```")):
        j -= 1
    while j > 0 and lines[j - 1].strip() and not lines[j - 1].strip().startswith("```") \
            and not FIELD_RE.match(lines[j - 1]) and not FIELD_RE.match(lines[j]):
        j -= 1
    return j


# keel's DND_HOLDS, verbatim (the suite compares them): the classes no unattended pick is taken in
HELD = (
    ("money in an option", re.compile(r"[$€£]\s?\d|\blist-only\b|\b(?:usd|eur|gbp)\b|\bsubscription\b|\binvoice\b", re.I)),
    ("a deletion", re.compile(r"\bdelet(?:e|es|ed|ing|ion)\b|\bdrop (?:the |a )?(?:table|database|repo)|\bwipe\b|\bpurge\b|\bforce-push", re.I)),
    ("a public publish", re.compile(r"\bpublish(?:es|ed|ing)?\b|\bmake (?:it |them )?public\b|\bgo(?:es)? public\b|\bopen[- ]source\b|\bpublic (?:twin|repo|release)\b", re.I)),
    ("a third party's answer", re.compile(r"\bthird[- ]part(?:y|ies)\b|\bvendor'?s? (?:answer|reply|approval)\b|\boutside (?:party|maintainer)\b|\btheir (?:answer|approval|reply|sign-off)\b", re.I)),
)
RECOMMENDED_RE = re.compile(r"(?i)^\W*(?:➤\s*)?[*_]*recommended[*_]*\s*:[*_`\s]*(.*)")   # bold or plain, at a line's start
REC_INLINE_RE = re.compile(r"(?i)➤\s*[*_]*recommended[*_]*\s*:[*_`\s]*(.*)")   # or after the options on the fix line, by its glyph
NO_PICK_RE = re.compile(r"(?i)^(?:none|n/?a|nothing|no pick|tbd|\?+)(?:\W|$)")   # a Recommended line that names no pick
# keel's REVERSIBLE_RE, a mark at a line's start only (never one the fix, the claim or the evidence quotes), and the
# same mark inside the Recommended line itself (`➤ Recommended: A — … Reversible: yes`). A `yes` must stand alone
# (`yes, as long as …` is a hedge: unmarked, keel's reversible_mark); a `no` may carry its reason
REVERSIBLE_RE = re.compile(r"^\s*(?:[-*]\s+)?[*_]*Reversible[*_]*\s*:[*_]*\s*[*_]*(yes|no)\b(.*)$", re.I)
REVERSIBLE_IN_RE = re.compile(r"[*_]*\bReversible[*_]*\s*:[*_]*\s*[*_]*(yes|no)\b(.*)$", re.I)
# a fix that names options: a choice, whether or not a pick is named. `A) …; B) …` (what the skill asks the verifier
# for), `(a) …`, `Option A`, `A. …`, and keel's own line shape `- A — …`; never `1) …; 2) …`, which reads as a fix's
# steps (round 2's verifier: every other shape read as no choice). The landing review added `Option 1`, a bare `a) …`
# and `A: …`; `either … or` on a block's own lines is a choice too (EITHER_RE)
OPTION_RES = (re.compile(r"(?:^|[\s;:(,])\(?([A-H])\)\s"), re.compile(r"\(([a-h])\)\s"), re.compile(r"\bOption\s+([A-H1-9])\b"),
              re.compile(r"(?:^|[\s;:,])([A-H])\.\s"), re.compile(r"^\s*(?:[-*]\s+)?\*{0,2}([A-H])\*{0,2}\s*[—–-]\s"),
              re.compile(r"(?:^|[;:]\s*)([a-h])\)\s"), re.compile(r"(?:^|[\s;:,(])([A-H]):\s"))
# `either … or` as a sentence's choice (`fix: either …`, `Either …`), never a condition inside a fix's prose
EITHER_RE = re.compile(r"(?:^\W*fix\W*:\s*(?i:either)|(?:^|[.;:]\s+)Either)\b.*\bor\b")
MODEL_COST_RE = re.compile(r"\$[\d.,]+[kKmM]?\s*\(\s*\$[\d.,]+[kKmM]?\s+effective\s*\)")   # keel's: a model cost is no money
# a pick that waits on something nobody settled: "if …", "unless …", "none until …", "A if …, else B"
CONDITIONAL_PICK_RE = re.compile(r"(?i)^(?:if|unless|when|none\s+until|depends)\b|\b(?:if|unless)\b.*\b(?:else|otherwise)\b")
# a pick is taken only when it names one listed option by its letter (keel's rule: a pick resolves to an option), what
# follows the letter carrying no hedge: `A if …`, `A unless …`, `A, once …`, `A — but B if …` all wait on something
# (the landing review: free text was taken whole, `(none)` and `the owner's call` included)
PICK_LETTER_RE = re.compile(r"^\**(?:Option\s+([A-H1-9])|\(([A-Ha-h])\)|([A-H]|[a-h](?=\))|[1-9])\)?)\**(?=$|[\s.,;:—–-])(.*)$",
                            re.S)
# a hedge is a word that opens the clause after the letter (`A if …`, `A, once …`, `A — but …`), never one deep in
# the reason (`A — nothing else waits`, `a lock, which holds when two runs race`: the fix review's corpus probe)
HEDGE_RE = re.compile(r"(?i)^[\s,—–-]*(?:\S+\s+){0,1}(?<!even )(?:if|unless|once|until|when|whenever|but|provided|assuming|depends|otherwise|else)\b")


def _blocks(text):
    """Each verdict block of a verifier's report: (head index, word, severity, start, end), lines read as
    `verdicts` reads them — a block from its head to the next block's head."""
    lines = text.splitlines()
    at = [i for i, line in enumerate(lines) if VERDICT_RE.match(line)]
    heads = [_head(lines, i) for i in at]
    out = []
    for n, i in enumerate(at):
        h = heads[n]
        sev = HEAD_SEV_RE.search(lines[h]) if h >= 0 else None
        end = heads[n + 1] if n + 1 < len(at) and heads[n + 1] > i else (at[n + 1] if n + 1 < len(at) else len(lines))
        out.append((h, VERDICT_RE.match(lines[i]).group(1).upper(), sev.group(1).upper() if sev else "", h + 1 if h >= 0 else 0, end))
    return lines, out


FIX_RE = re.compile(r"(?i)^\W*fix\W*:\W*(.*)")


def _field(lines, start, end, rx=EVIDENCE_RE):
    """(first, past) line indexes of the block's field rx, its wrapped lines included; None for none."""
    for k in range(start, end):
        if not rx.match(lines[k]):
            continue
        m = k + 1
        while m < end and lines[m].strip() and not FIELD_RE.match(lines[m]) and not lines[m].strip().startswith("```"):
            m += 1
        return k, m
    return None


def _evidence(lines, start, end, rx=EVIDENCE_RE):
    """The block's evidence (or another field, rx), wrapped lines joined; "" for none."""
    f = _field(lines, start, end, rx)
    if not f:
        return ""
    body = [rx.match(lines[f[0]]).group(1).strip()] + [lines[m].strip() for m in range(f[0] + 1, f[1])]
    return " ".join(x for x in body if x)


def reversible_mark(m):
    """'yes', 'no' or None (unmarked, a hedged yes) from a REVERSIBLE_RE or REVERSIBLE_IN_RE match: keel's rule."""
    if not m:
        return None
    word, rest = m.group(1).lower(), m.group(2).strip().strip("*_.").strip()
    return None if word == "yes" and rest else word


def picks(text):
    """[(kind, head, detail)] for each real finding in a verifier's report: fix, take or stop (the header's `picks`)."""
    lines, blocks = _blocks(text)
    out = []
    for h, word, sev, start, end in blocks:
        ev = _evidence(lines, start, end)
        if word != "WRONG" or sev == "S3" or not ev or NO_EVIDENCE_RE.match(ev):
            continue
        head = lines[h].strip() if h >= 0 else "(no head)"
        ev_span = _field(lines, start, end) or (end, end)
        # the block bar its head and its evidence: the claim and the evidence may well name a deletion the defect
        # makes, while the options, wherever they sit (a blank line may part them from `fix:`), are what a pick does
        own = [k for k in range(start, end) if not ev_span[0] <= k < ev_span[1]]
        at = next((k for k in own if RECOMMENDED_RE.match(lines[k])), None)
        if at is None:
            at = next((k for k in own if REC_INLINE_RE.search(lines[k])), None)
        # the Recommended line's own text after its label is the pick, never an option it lists (`A: quote it`)
        cut = {at: (RECOMMENDED_RE.match(lines[at]) or REC_INLINE_RE.search(lines[at])).start(1)} if at is not None else {}
        options = {m.group(1).upper() for k in own for rx in OPTION_RES for m in rx.finditer(lines[k][:cut.get(k, None)])}
        if at is None:
            marked = any(REVERSIBLE_RE.match(lines[k]) for k in own)
            either = any(EITHER_RE.search(lines[k]) for k in own)
            out.append(("stop", head, "a choice with no ➤ Recommended: pick") if len(options) > 1 or marked or either
                       else ("fix", head, ""))
            continue
        rec = (RECOMMENDED_RE.match(lines[at]) or REC_INLINE_RE.search(lines[at])).group(1).strip()
        inline = REVERSIBLE_IN_RE.search(rec)
        pick = (rec[:inline.start()] if inline else rec).strip(" .;,—-*_`")
        pick = "" if NO_PICK_RE.match(pick) else pick
        # the mark on the Recommended line, else the first line-start mark after it: never one before it (an option's)
        marks = ([inline] if inline else []) + [m for k in own if k > at for m in [REVERSIBLE_RE.match(lines[k])] if m]
        mark = marks[0] if marks else None
        rev = reversible_mark(mark)
        # two marks that disagree (an inline yes, then a `Reversible: no` line): no pick is taken on either
        contradicts = len({reversible_mark(m) for m in marks}) > 1
        lm = PICK_LETTER_RE.match(pick) if pick else None
        letter = next((g for g in lm.groups()[:3] if g), "").upper() if lm else ""
        hedged = bool(lm) and bool(HEDGE_RE.search(re.split(r"[;.]", lm.group(4), maxsplit=1)[0]))
        # options written in the evidence are what the pick does too, so the held classes read them there
        ev_lines = [lines[k] for k in range(*ev_span) if k < end]
        ev_opts = {m.group(1).upper() for x in ev_lines for rx in OPTION_RES for m in rx.finditer(x)}
        options |= ev_opts if len(ev_opts) > 1 else set()
        does = MODEL_COST_RE.sub(" ", "\n".join([lines[k] for k in own] + (ev_lines if len(ev_opts) > 1 else [])))
        held = next(("%s (%s)" % (why, m.group(0)) for why, rx in HELD for m in [rx.search(does)] if m), None)
        why = ("no pick named after ➤ Recommended:" if not pick else
               "Reversible: no" if rev == "no" else
               "contradictory Reversible: marks" if contradicts else
               "not marked Reversible: yes|no (a hedged yes is none)" if rev is None else
               "a conditional pick (%s)" % pick[:60] if CONDITIONAL_PICK_RE.search(pick) or hedged else
               "the pick names no listed option (%s)" % pick[:60] if not letter or letter not in options else
               held)
        out.append(("stop", head, why) if why else ("take", head, pick))
    return out, len(blocks)


def verdicts(text):
    """(raised, confirmed, open_s1) from a verifier's report in the reviewer agents' format (`file:line — [S2] claim`,
    then `verdict:` and `evidence:` lines): every verdict block is raised; a WRONG with evidence and no [S3] head is
    confirmed; an UNVERIFIABLE with an [S1] head is open_s1, which `covers` will not vouch past. A WRONG whose
    evidence is empty or says there is none is a theory (the reviewer's own words), never a real finding. Evidence
    may wrap onto the lines after its label; a block ends at the next block's head, so it never borrows another's."""
    lines = text.splitlines()
    at = [i for i, line in enumerate(lines) if VERDICT_RE.match(line)]
    heads = [_head(lines, i) for i in at]
    raised = confirmed = open_s1 = 0
    for n, i in enumerate(at):
        raised += 1
        word = VERDICT_RE.match(lines[i]).group(1).upper()
        h = heads[n]
        sev = HEAD_SEV_RE.search(lines[h]) if h >= 0 else None
        sev = sev.group(1).upper() if sev else ""
        if word == "UNVERIFIABLE" and sev == "S1":
            open_s1 += 1
        if word != "WRONG" or sev == "S3":
            continue
        # this block: its head to the next block's head (evidence may sit before the verdict line too)
        end = heads[n + 1] if n + 1 < len(at) and heads[n + 1] > i else (at[n + 1] if n + 1 < len(at) else len(lines))
        start = h + 1 if h >= 0 else 0
        for k in range(start, end):
            e = EVIDENCE_RE.match(lines[k])
            if not e:
                continue
            body = [e.group(1).strip()]
            for m in range(k + 1, end):
                if not lines[m].strip() or FIELD_RE.match(lines[m]) or lines[m].strip().startswith("```"):
                    break
                body.append(lines[m].strip())
            ev = " ".join(x for x in body if x)
            confirmed += bool(ev) and not NO_EVIDENCE_RE.match(ev)
            break
    return raised, confirmed, open_s1


class Once(argparse.Action):
    """--phase given once: a landing's allow rule names `round <worktree> --phase finish` (autofinish-run's
    FINISH_TOOLS), and argparse keeps the last of a repeated flag, so `--phase finish --phase build` (or an
    abbreviation, `--ph build`) would pass the rule and write the build's record (AGENTS.md Traps: a gate that
    judges a command line refuses a gated flag given twice)."""

    def __call__(self, parser, namespace, values, option_string=None):
        if getattr(namespace, "_phase_given", False):
            parser.error("--phase given twice: once only")
        namespace._phase_given = True
        setattr(namespace, self.dest, values)


def count(v):
    if v < 0:
        raise argparse.ArgumentTypeError("a count is 0 or more")
    return v


def main(argv):
    ap = argparse.ArgumentParser(prog="build-review", description=__doc__.split("\n\n")[0])
    sub = ap.add_subparsers(dest="verb", required=True)
    vr = sub.add_parser("review")
    vr.add_argument("worktree")
    vr.add_argument("--cli", choices=("codex", "antigravity"), required=True)
    vr.add_argument("--out", default="")
    vr.add_argument("--base", default="origin/main")
    vr.add_argument("--origin", default="")
    r = sub.add_parser("round")
    r.add_argument("worktree")
    r.add_argument("--found", type=lambda s: count(int(s)), default=None)   # 0 alone: a count above comes from --verdicts
    r.add_argument("--verdicts", default=None)
    r.add_argument("--fixed", type=lambda s: count(int(s)), required=True)
    r.add_argument("--left", type=lambda s: count(int(s)), default=None)   # unset: found - fixed
    r.add_argument("--check", choices=("green", "red"))
    r.add_argument("--reviewers", default="")
    r.add_argument("--note", default="")
    r.add_argument("--phase", choices=PHASES, default="build", action=Once)
    e = sub.add_parser("end")
    e.add_argument("worktree")
    e.add_argument("--outcome", choices=OUTCOMES, required=True)
    e.add_argument("--why", default="")
    e.add_argument("--phase", choices=PHASES, default="build", action=Once)
    s = sub.add_parser("show")
    s.add_argument("worktree")
    s.add_argument("--phase", choices=PHASES, default="build")
    g = sub.add_parser("gate")
    g.add_argument("worktree")
    g.add_argument("--session", required=True)
    c = sub.add_parser("compare")
    c.add_argument("--json", action="store_true")
    v = sub.add_parser("covers")
    v.add_argument("worktree")
    v.add_argument("--base", default="origin/main")
    sub.add_parser("cap")
    k = sub.add_parser("picks")
    k.add_argument("file")
    k.add_argument("worktree")
    a = ap.parse_args(argv)
    if a.verb == "picks":
        try:
            text = Path(a.file).read_text(encoding="utf-8", errors="replace")
        except OSError as err:
            print("build-review: the verdict file does not read (%s)" % err, file=sys.stderr)
            return 2
        where(a.worktree)
        rows, n = picks(text)
        if not n:
            print("build-review: no `verdict:` line in %s: the verifier's report, verbatim, one block per item" % a.file,
                  file=sys.stderr)
            return 2
        for kind, head, detail in rows:
            print("%s %s%s" % (kind, head, " — " + detail if detail else ""))
        if not rows:
            print("none: no real finding")
        return 5 if any(r[0] == "stop" for r in rows) else 0
    if a.verb == "review":
        import vendorlib
        import vendorreview
        try:
            out = a.out or str(where(a.worktree)[0].parent / ("vendor-review-%d" % time.time_ns()))
            print(json.dumps(vendorreview.review(a.worktree, a.cli, out, a.base, a.origin)))
            return 0
        except (vendorlib.Refused, OSError, ValueError) as ex:
            print("build-review: %s" % ex, file=sys.stderr)
            return 2
    if a.verb == "cap":
        print(rounds_cap())
        return 0
    if a.verb == "covers":
        ok, why = covers(a.worktree, a.base)
        print(("covered: " if ok else "not covered: ") + why)
        return 0 if ok else 1
    if a.verb == "compare":
        rows = compare(log_path().parent)
        if a.json:
            print(json.dumps(rows, indent=2))
            return 0
        if not rows:
            print("no ended build-review record in %s" % log_path())
            return 0
        print("%-46s %-14s %6s %9s  %-26s %9s %9s" % ("branch", "outcomes", "rounds", "found/fix", "finish S1/S2/S3 wrong",
                                                     "build $", "finish $"))
        for b in rows:
            fin = "; ".join("skipped" if f.get("skipped") else "%d/%d/%d %d" % (f["S1"], f["S2"], f["S3"], f["wrong"])
                            for f in b["finish"]) or "none yet"
            print("%-46s %-14s %6d %9s  %-26s %9.2f %9.2f" % (
                ("%s:%s" % (b["project"], b["branch"].rsplit("/", 1)[-1]))[:46], ",".join(b["outcomes"])[:14], b["rounds"],
                "%d/%d" % (b["found"], b["fixed"]), fin[:26], b["spend"]["build"], b["spend"]["finish"]))
        fin = [b for b in rows if b["finish"]]
        # the first pass, never the last: a landing that loops logs a pass per round, and its last is the clean one after
        # its fixes, which would hide what the landing review found on a build-reviewed branch (the landing review)
        last = [b["finish"][0] for b in fin]
        skip = sum(1 for f in last if f.get("skipped"))
        print("%d build(s), %d with a finish review%s: its first pass raised S1 %d, S2 %d, WRONG %d (raw, unjudged); "
              "review spend $%.2f in the builds, $%.2f in the finishes (list)" % (
                  len(rows), len(fin), " (%d skipped)" % skip if skip else "", sum(f["S1"] for f in last), sum(f["S2"] for f in last), sum(f["wrong"] for f in last),
                  sum(b["spend"]["build"] for b in rows), sum(b["spend"]["finish"] for b in fin)))
        return 0

    raised = open_s1 = 0
    if a.verb == "round":
        # before the record is touched: a refused round writes nothing (the header's "Real is the verifier's word")
        if a.verdicts is not None and a.found is not None:
            print("build-review: --found or --verdicts, never both: the verifier's file is the count", file=sys.stderr)
            return 2
        if a.verdicts is None and a.found is None:
            print("build-review: a round needs --verdicts <the verifier's report>, or --found 0 when the review raised no "
                  "S1, S2 or WRONG item", file=sys.stderr)
            return 2
        if a.found:
            print("build-review: --found %d: a real finding is the verifier's word, never the builder's; spawn a fresh "
                  "reviewer on the round's S1, S2 and WRONG items and pass its report as --verdicts <file>" % a.found,
                  file=sys.stderr)
            return 2
        if a.verdicts is not None:
            try:
                raised, a.found, open_s1 = verdicts(Path(a.verdicts).read_text(encoding="utf-8", errors="replace"))
            except OSError as err:
                print("build-review: the verdict file does not read (%s)" % err, file=sys.stderr)
                return 2
            if not raised:
                print("build-review: no `verdict:` line in %s: the verifier's report, verbatim, one block per item"
                      % a.verdicts, file=sys.stderr)
                return 2
    phase = getattr(a, "phase", "build")
    path, branch, repo = where(a.worktree, phase)
    d = load(path)
    if a.verb == "gate":
        if not d or not a.session or d.get("session") != a.session or d.get("branch") != branch:
            return 0
        if d.get("ended") and d.get("outcome") == "clean":
            return 0
        if d.get("ended"):
            print("its review loop ended %s%s" % (d.get("outcome"), ": %s" % d["why"] if d.get("why") else ""))
            return 1
        if d.get("rounds"):
            print("its review loop recorded %d round(s) and never ended" % len(d["rounds"]))
            return 1
        return 0
    session = os.environ.get("CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID", "")
    if a.verb != "show" and not session:
        print("build-review: no CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID: run it from the build's own session", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    if a.verb in ("round", "end") and phase == "build":
        # the landing's allow rule names `--phase finish` by the command's text, which a quoted value or a `#` can hold
        # while argparse reads the default: the finish's own session never writes the build's record (the landing
        # review: a forged clean build record, which a later landing's covers would trust)
        af = load(Path(git(a.worktree, "rev-parse", "--absolute-git-dir") or ".") / "claude" / "autofinish.json")
        if af and af.get("session") == session and not af.get("ended"):
            print("build-review: this session is the landing's run (autofinish.json): its loop is --phase finish, never "
                  "the build's record", file=sys.stderr)
            return 2
    pf = " --phase finish" if phase == "finish" else ""
    fresh = {"branch": branch, "session": session, "repo": repo, "project": os.path.basename(repo),
             "worktree": git(a.worktree, "rev-parse", "--show-toplevel") or "", "started": int(time.time()), "rounds": []}
    if phase == "finish":   # the build's lines carry no key: a reader takes a line without one as the build's
        fresh["phase"] = "finish"
    elif os.environ.get("REGATTA_BUILD_AGENT") in ("codex", "antigravity"):
        fresh["agent"] = os.environ["REGATTA_BUILD_AGENT"]
    # this run's record, or a fresh one: an ended record, another branch's, or another session's (a run that
    # died before `end`) never lends its rounds to the cap or a second log line (the build-review review)
    same = bool(d) and d.get("branch") == branch and d.get("session", "") == session
    # one loop per session: after its end, neither a second end nor a new round (a capped run must not
    # restart its own cap, nor log its build twice; the round-3 review)
    reopened = False
    if a.verb in ("round", "end") and same and d.get("ended") and d.get("outcome") != "held":
        print("build-review: this session ended the record already (%s): one loop, one end, per session" % d.get("outcome"),
              file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    if a.verb in ("round", "end") and same and d.get("ended"):
        # a held loop reopened by the session the answer woke: its rounds still count against the cap, and its next
        # end writes a second log line for the same (session, started), which a reader takes the last of. An end
        # reopens it too: a held loop at the cap must still be able to end cap (the finish review)
        for k in ("ended", "outcome", "why", "tip"):
            d.pop(k, None)
        d["reopened"] = d.get("reopened", 0) + 1
        reopened = True
    mine = same and not d.get("ended")
    if a.verb != "show" and not mine:
        if d and not d.get("ended") and d.get("rounds"):
            # another session's loop that never ended (the run died): logged as `died`, never lost
            log({**d, "ended": int(time.time()), "outcome": "died", "why": "replaced by session %s" % session})
        d = fresh
        # saved at once: a call refused below (a clean end with no round) must not leave the dead record to be
        # logged `died` again by the next call (the finish review)
        save(path, d)
    if d:
        d.setdefault("rounds", [])
    if a.verb == "show":
        print(json.dumps(d, indent=2) if d else "none")
        return 0
    if a.verb == "round":
        n, cap = len(d["rounds"]) + 1, rounds_cap()
        prev = d["rounds"][-1] if d["rounds"] else None
        # granted only on a confirmed finding or a red check; a round with no --check recorded grants nothing either.
        # A held loop reopened by the answer's session (or a round that left items) is the D-item road: its next round
        # reviews the answer's code, whatever the round before found (the verified-rounds landing review). Asked
        # before the cap, so a clean loop at the cap is told to end clean, not cap.
        # the final-tip rule (the header): a round that confirmed nothing still grants one more when the code moved past
        # what its review read (a fix commit after the review), so the loop's last review reads the tip it hands over
        prose = phase == "finish" and in_place(a.worktree, repo, base_ref(a.worktree, repo))
        # a finish round with no logged head: no review read anything this end could vouch for, so one more round is
        # granted, as `end` refuses its clean end (the fix review: the two disagreed and the loop could not move)
        moved = bool(prev) and (branch_moved(a.worktree, prev["reviewed"], base_ref(a.worktree, repo), prose=prose) is not False if prev.get("reviewed")
                                else phase == "finish")
        if prev and not reopened and not moved and not prev.get("left") and not prev.get("found") and prev.get("check") != "red":
            print("build-review: round %d confirmed no real finding and its check was %s: no further round is "
                  "granted; end the record (build-review end %s%s --outcome clean%s)" % (
                      prev.get("round", n - 1), prev.get("check") or "not recorded", a.worktree, pf,
                      "" if prev.get("check") == "green" else ", after a round with --check green"),
                  file=sys.stderr)
            return 4
        if n > cap and moved and prev and not reopened and not prev.get("left") and not prev.get("found") \
                and prev.get("check") == "green":
            ok = phase == "build" or (not prose and tests_only_move(a.worktree, prev.get("reviewed"), repo))
            print("build-review: %d rounds already, the cap, and no round is left to review the move since %s: end the "
                  "record %s" % (cap, (prev.get("reviewed") or "")[:9],
                                 "clean (build-review end %s --outcome clean): /finish reviews the move" % a.worktree
                                 if phase == "build" else
                                 "clean (--phase finish --outcome clean): a tests-only move" if ok else
                                 "cap (--phase finish --outcome cap): no later review reads it"), file=sys.stderr)
            return 3
        if n > cap:
            print("build-review: %d rounds already, the cap: fix nothing more in this run; end the record "
                  "(build-review end %s%s --outcome cap) and end naming what is left" % (cap, a.worktree, pf),
                  file=sys.stderr)
            return 3
        reviewed = build_reviewed_head(d["project"], branch, time.time() + 60, phase)
        d["rounds"].append({"round": n, "tip": git(a.worktree, "rev-parse", "HEAD") or "", "at": int(time.time()),
                            "reviewed": reviewed,
                            "reviewers": [x.strip() for x in a.reviewers.split(",") if x.strip()], "raised": raised, "found": a.found,
                            "open_s1": open_s1,
                            "fixed": a.fixed, "left": max(a.found - a.fixed, 0) if a.left is None else a.left, "check": a.check, "note": a.note})
        if a.verdicts:
            d["rounds"][-1]["findings"] = Path(a.verdicts).read_text(encoding="utf-8", errors="replace")
        save(path, d)
        # clean only when the round found nothing: a round whose findings were all fixed needs one more
        # review of the fixes (the brief: "until a pass finds nothing to fix and the check is green")
        clean = a.found == 0 and a.check == "green"
        stale = (branch_moved(a.worktree, reviewed, base_ref(a.worktree, repo), prose=prose) is not False) if reviewed else phase == "finish"
        print("build-review: round %d of %d recorded%s" % (n, cap,
              (" — nothing found, but the code moved since its review read %s: one more round reviews the tip"
               % ((reviewed or "")[:9] or "no logged head")) if clean and stale and n < cap
              else " — nothing found and the check green: end it (--outcome clean)" if clean and (not stale or phase == "build")
              else "" if n < cap else
              " — nothing found, but the code moved since its review read %s, at the cap: end it clean only on a "
              "tests-only move, else cap" % ((reviewed or "")[:9] or "no logged head") if clean
              else " — the cap: end it (--outcome cap) and name what is left"))
        return 0
    # end
    last = d["rounds"][-1] if d.get("rounds") else None
    if a.outcome == "clean" and not (last and last.get("found") == 0 and last.get("check") == "green"):
        print("build-review: a clean end wants a last round that found nothing with the check green (%s): record that "
              "round first, or end cap or held" % ("no round yet" if not last else "found %s, check %s" % (last.get("found"), last.get("check"))),
              file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    open_s1 = sum(r.get("open_s1") or 0 for r in d.get("rounds") or [])
    if a.outcome == "clean" and phase == "finish" and open_s1:
        # an S1 nobody could confirm or refute, in any round (a later round's review never re-verifies it): the build's
        # covers sends it to /finish's review, and a finish has no later review, so it holds the landing (round 2's
        # verifier; the landing review: an earlier round's was forgotten)
        print("build-review: not clean: the loop's rounds left %d S1 its verifier called UNVERIFIABLE, and no later review "
              "reads it: end held (--phase finish --outcome held --why '<the S1>'), naming it in the fix round's brief"
              % open_s1, file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    if a.outcome == "clean":
        prose = phase == "finish" and in_place(a.worktree, repo, base_ref(a.worktree, repo))
        dirt = dirty_paths(a.worktree, prose)
        if dirt is None or dirt:
            print("build-review: not clean: %s: commit it, then one more round reviews it" % (
                "git cannot read the worktree's status" if dirt is None else
                "the worktree holds uncommitted changes no review read (%s)" % ", ".join(dirt[:3])), file=sys.stderr)
            return 2
    if a.outcome == "clean":
        # the final-tip rule (the header): clean means the last recorded round's review read the code this end hands
        # over — never a review logged after it, whose findings no round verified (the landing review). A build round
        # with no head keeps main's reading (no log, no rule); a finish loop always follows a review, so none there
        # means the log was not read, and it is no clean end
        head = last.get("reviewed")
        moved = branch_moved(a.worktree, head, base_ref(a.worktree, repo), prose=prose) if head else (None if phase == "finish" else False)
        at_cap = len(d.get("rounds") or []) >= rounds_cap()
        if head and moved is not False and at_cap and (phase == "build" or (not prose and tests_only_move(a.worktree, head, repo))):
            # no round is left to review the move (the round-1 verifier's finding): the build ends clean as on main, and
            # `covers` then sees the moved code and sends /finish to review it; a finish's own loop has no later review,
            # so only a tests-only move (a check fix, covers' own rule) passes there
            print("build-review: the code moved since the newest %s review read %s, at the cap: ended clean%s" % (
                phase, head[:9], ", and /finish reviews the move (covers says no)" if phase == "build"
                else " on a tests-only move (a check fix)"), file=sys.stderr)
            moved = False
        if moved is not False:
            print("build-review: not clean: the last round's %s review read %s, and the code at HEAD %s: %s" % (
                      phase, (head or "")[:9] or "no logged head", "differs from it" if moved else "cannot be compared with it",
                      "end cap: no round is left under the cap to review it" if at_cap else
                      "run one more round (it is granted) so the last review reads the tip, or end cap or held"),
                  file=sys.stderr)
            return 2
    d.update(ended=int(time.time()), outcome=a.outcome, why=a.why,
             tip=git(a.worktree, "rev-parse", "HEAD") or "")
    # the log first, and no save without it: a record saved ended and never logged could not be ended again
    if not log(d, path):
        print("build-review: not ended: the record stays open, end it again once the log is writable", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    save(path, d)
    print("build-review: ended %s after %d round(s)" % (a.outcome, len(d["rounds"])))
    return 0


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main(sys.argv[1:]))
